From 04c29ab339550294f36873862c5da4c537e85ee3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nic Boet <nic@boet.cc>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 18:42:09 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] manual: do not parse the pasted authenticator data as CBOR

In manual mode, manual_get_assert() base64-decodes the authenticator
data the user pastes and hands it to fido_assert_set_authdata(), which
parses it with libcbor's cbor_load(). libcbor preallocates definite
arrays and maps from the header count before reading any element
(PJK/libcbor#418), so a short pasted value makes the PAM process
request gigabytes. Over sshd keyboard-interactive this is reachable by
a remote user before any credential is accepted.

Unwrap the CBOR byte string locally (definite length, exactly the
remaining input, nothing else accepted) and pass the payload to
fido_assert_set_authdata_raw(), available since libfido2 1.3.0, which
builds its own well-formed encoding. Bytes typed by the user never
reach cbor_load(). Valid input decodes exactly as before.

No allocator is installed and no process limit is changed, unlike the
approaches declined in #394 and Yubico/libfido2#985.

tests/manual_authdata.c covers the one-, two-, three-, five- and
nine-byte header forms, rejects other major types, indefinite and
reserved headers, length mismatches and truncation, and shows with a
guard allocator that the new path requests nothing large where the
old one did.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Mythos 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
